//BUG="a struct field with a negative reference depth panics the type resolver"
//ERR any

// `|a` dereferences an i32. The type resolver has no error for that; it
// records the result as "an i32 with a reference depth of -1" and carries on
// (68e is what that leads to when the value is thrown away).
//
// Putting the value into a tuple — or any struct literal — makes the resolver
// finish the struct's type. `run_queue` (src/compiler/type_resolver/mod.rs)
// does that as soon as every field counts as known, and "known" is
// `check_is_type_known`: the field has a base type and its depth is fixed.
// Both hold for -1. It then assembles the struct with
//
//     struct_fields.push(self.fetch_final_ir_type(type_label).unwrap());
//
// and `fetch_final_ir_type` returns `None` for a negative depth, because
// there is no such type. The compiler panics:
//
//     called `Option::unwrap()` on a `None` value
//
// Without the tuple the same slip is at least an error, though not one that
// says what is wrong: `b = |a` reports "Could not deduce this expression's
// type", and 07_references/52e gets "No candidate found for this function
// call" from the `out` that uses the value.
//
// A reference depth that is fixed and negative is a contradiction the moment
// it arises, in `merge_ref`. Reporting it there — "this is not a reference"
// — fixes this panic, gives 68e its error, and replaces both of the
// misleading messages above. There is no `//MSG` because that diagnostic does
// not exist yet.

fn main
    a = 5
    t = |a, 1
    out "ok"
